00:02:59.955 --> 00:03:02.100
Like, what can I do with this?

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.590
And this is where I get
really excited because we

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.200
can use all those
billion devices

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.590
in these different languages
to really expose our content

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.910
to other users as well.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.960
And so I'd like to start off
with just some terminology.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.960
Because we use the same
logo for Google Assistant,

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.720
which is a conversation
between Google and users.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.090
And then there are
the actual devices.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.160
That's the hardware that exists.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.290
And Actions on Google
is the platform

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.000
that allows developers to
extend the Google Assistant

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.282
experience.

00:03:33.282 --> 00:03:33.990
NETO MARIN: Yeah.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.550
I'd like to explain
this picture.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:36.950
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: Exactly.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.200
NETO MARIN: For example, I
have an Android development

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.260
background and full stack
development background.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.140
So at the beginning,
it took me time

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:47.340
to understand where the
components are fitting

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.180
together.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.055
So for example, here in
the Google Assistant,

00:03:50.055 --> 00:03:53.580
you can imagine this as the
web or the Android platform,

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.340
where all the apps
or your web apps

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.510
live, where is the
actions on Google.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.560
Sorry-- the devices that have
it working, like your phone.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.570
And the apps are the actions.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.780
So it's just a
new name for apps.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.610
It's like instead of
saying I'm developing

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.370
app, an application
for Google Assistant,

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:14.973
we're developing actions.

00:04:14.973 --> 00:04:16.390
Because we believe
that you're not

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.500
developing only the
experience of running an app,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.670
but much more.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.680
You are interacting
as an action.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.320
You are running an action
using the Google Assistant.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.410
So I like to establish
the comparison

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.390
if you want to keep in mind.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.190
Google Assistant's
your platform.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.800
Actions on Google's your apps.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.180
And devices are your
smartphones, or your browser.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.638
It depends on where
you compare to.

00:04:39.638 --> 00:04:40.930
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: Definitely.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.920
So Actions on Google is just
the platform in which developers

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.130
can build applications-- or
actions that we call it within

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.630
our world--

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:51.490
to expose that content and
to have that functionality.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.075
So how does it all work?

00:04:53.075 --> 00:04:55.200
So we're going to start
off on the right hand side.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.890
Imagine Neto speaking this.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:00.750
He's saying something like,
OK, G, talk to Personal Chef.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.680
It's a made up
action that we made.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.240
And so what happens there is
the device hears the keyword,

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.730
grabs the audio, and sends that
audio over to Google Assistant.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:12.078
Google Assistant will
translate that audio to text.

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:14.370
We're going to use our own
natural language processing.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.078
We're going to use
the user's preference.

00:05:16.078 --> 00:05:18.960
We do our own machine learning
ranking, as well as the user

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.960
profile, to figure out
what does Neto actually

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.330
want when he says that?

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:26.520
And because he explicitly said
the name of an action that

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.560
exists, what's
going to happen is

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.420
Google Assistant is going
to ping my action's back end

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.250
and go, hey, someone wants
to talk to your action.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.270
NETO MARIN: Exactly.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:37.312
And this is the cool point,
because, for example,

00:05:37.312 --> 00:05:40.130
in this first part, you can--

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:43.580
since the beginning, you can
handle all the communication

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.360
with your service.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.350
So if you have, for example,
premium service, premium

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:52.060
entitlements, or you would like
to check the person is logged

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.040
in or not, you can
start right away

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.790
from the first interaction.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:59.940
So because the Google
Assistant, in this case,

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:04.310
it's working more as a
bridge between the user

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.370
and your application.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:08.330
It's like you take your
phone, open the launcher,

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.730
and selecting one app is
what you are doing here.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.100
You are saying to
access that app--

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.400
this action, in this case.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:14.890
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA:
Yeah, definitely.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:16.790
Because after that,
if you notice,

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:19.790
Google Assistant doesn't do much
after it makes the connection.

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:21.470
After that, it's all on us.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.780
So we create the
response we want to give,

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:28.190
and we give that response in
a text form, in a JSON format.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:30.020
And we send it over
to Google Assistant.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:32.780
Google Assistant will then will
translate that text to speech,

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.000
and then send the audio
over to the device

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.730
and actually respond
back to Neto.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.020
NETO MARIN: Yes, exactly.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.200
And the cool stuff you
might be thinking like, OK,

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.770
so on my back end, I
have to do, for example,

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:48.380
a bunch of natural
language processing

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.380
stuff to understand
what the user said

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:55.850
and then identify
what the user wants.

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:57.008
Not many work, right?

00:06:57.008 --> 00:06:58.300
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: Oh my gosh.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:58.800
Yeah.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.590
Users are unique and different.

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:03.693
I know for myself, if you
were to ask my partner,

00:07:03.693 --> 00:07:05.360
he would definitely
contest that I'm not

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.340
the most articulate person,
especially when it's things

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.580
that I want and my needs.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.010
And let me put in my password.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:15.793
So then in processing users--

00:07:15.793 --> 00:07:16.460
NETO MARIN: Yes.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.230
For example, in
this case, what we

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:20.390
are saying that you
don't need to implement

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:23.720
your own natural language
processing to handle

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.420
different accents,
different languages.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.760
We have a tool for
you to do that.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.160
It's Dialogflow.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:34.100
And please raise
the hand who already

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:35.567
knows what Dialogflow is.

00:07:35.567 --> 00:07:36.650
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: Sweet.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.350
Dozen.

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:41.520
NETO MARIN: So basically,
it's like intent matching

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:44.210
[? and intent ?] [? reaction. ?]
So Dialogflow is doing all

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:48.110
the work for you in terms of
identifying words the user

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:52.100
said, the intent, or what
the user wants you to do.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:55.480
And then you match exactly
to the part of her code.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.380
And this is cool
because, for example, you

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:00.200
don't need to have
different back ends

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.460
for different languages.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:04.100
We're going to go through
this on other examples.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:07.250
You can have your only
one single back end

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:09.980
that you handle all the
requests because everything

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:11.150
would be handled--

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:13.840
in terms of language and
processing-- by Dialogflow.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:14.840
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: Yes.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.942
So where does that leave within
our kind of flow of data?

00:08:17.942 --> 00:08:18.650
NETO MARIN: Yeah.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.483
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: So
that lives, actually,

00:08:20.483 --> 00:08:22.450
between the Google
Assistant and your back end.

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.020
And so it lives
actually right here.

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:25.820
And what Dialogflow
is going to do,

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:28.523
it's going to get that raw
text of the user's utterance,

00:08:28.523 --> 00:08:30.440
and it's going to use
its own natural language

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.480
processing to figure
out what do they

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:35.960
mean when they say a statement,
and then try to match it

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:38.720
with predefined
functionalities, as a developer,

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:39.836
we have defined.

00:08:39.836 --> 00:08:41.419
And if it doesn't
match with anything,

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:43.559
we have a default
fallback intent,

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:45.800
which I like to think as
the else statement of all

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:46.970
my intents.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:48.680
And it will be
captured through there.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.570
And so Dialogflow just
sits right between.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.790
And then it hits
our code going, hey,

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:56.220
they're hitting this part of
your function, this function.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.830
And here are any
of the parameters

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:01.020
that you have asked me
to send to you as well.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:03.187
And then at that point,
we just make our queries

00:09:03.187 --> 00:09:05.270
to our database, do our
jazz, create the response,

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:06.930
and then send that
back to Dialogflow.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.570
Dialogflow to Google Assistant.

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:10.610
Google Assistant translates
that text to speech.

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:12.860
And then that audio
gets sent to the device.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.420
NETO MARIN: And this is
less than milliseconds.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:19.610
So we don't have to worry
about overhead timing.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:21.650
Because when the
user asks something,

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:25.730
you have kind of eight
seconds to reply.

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:27.650
So if you don't reply
in eight seconds,

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.080
the connection will
be closed as time out.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.510
Because, of course, we
don't want to have actions

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:33.870
listen to you all the time.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.590
So it will be closed
in eight seconds.

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:40.820
And you might think, OK, you
have Assistant, Dialogflow,

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:44.150
my back end, reading
data, returning this

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:45.950
all the way around.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:48.840
Yeah, it will take less
then a few milliseconds.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.458
So you can focus
on your own code.

00:09:53.458 --> 00:09:54.500
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: Cool.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.500
So we wanted to talk
about-- seeing that there's

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:00.620
some people who have
used Dialogflow,

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:03.020
we really wanted to talk
about localizing it.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.770
Because it's one thing
to build an action,

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.390
especially in your
own native language.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:06.810
You build it.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:07.790
You're like, this is awesome.

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:08.998
It has all the functionality.

00:10:08.998 --> 00:10:10.040
It's doing what I want.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:13.670
And now the next thing
is, how do I expose this

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:14.870
to so many other people?

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:16.790
Because it's on the
billion devices.

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:19.215
But if somebody is
at their own home

00:10:19.215 --> 00:10:20.840
and they're only
speaking one language,

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:22.350
you won't have access to them.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:26.935
So what we did for this
particular action is for GDG,

00:10:26.935 --> 00:10:28.310
which I believe
they were talking

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:32.000
about that in the talk prior,
about Google Developer Groups.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.280
And so this is a project
that I've been building out

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:38.030
where a Google Developer
Group organizer can just

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:39.890
connect to their meetup API.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:42.502
And it has a fully
functioned action

00:10:42.502 --> 00:10:44.210
that will provide
information like when's

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.127
the next meetup, when's
the last meetup, who's

00:10:46.127 --> 00:10:47.960
the organizer, how
many people are there.

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:51.410
And it's super easy
for our GDGs to set up.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:54.910
And so the first thing I did
to make it localized was--

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:55.880
let's see.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:00.560
Next-- was, of course,
the way that we have our--

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.560
I'm using Node.js because
we have our client library

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:04.910
in a language in JavaScript.

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:06.690
And so have an index.js file.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:08.813
And I had all my code and
all my responses there.

00:11:08.813 --> 00:11:10.730
And so the first thing
I needed to do in order

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:14.360
to localize my action was I
needed to actually pull out

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.100
all the responses.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:18.350
And so I just separate
it, have all my responses

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.980
in the other file, and I
pulled on it whenever I wanted.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.710
This really helped me
think about the responses

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:27.530
of my action in big categories.

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:29.930
Because I had defined,
this is the greeting.

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:31.700
This is the greeting
for new users

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:33.502
versus the greeting
of returning users,

00:11:33.502 --> 00:11:35.210
which is naturally
going to be different.

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:38.090
Because when I first met
Neto, I had to go, hey, Neto,

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:39.610
I'm Jessica.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:40.890
I'm from Calexico.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:42.140
This is kind of my background.

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.450
I gave him a [INAUDIBLE]
the first time I meet him.

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:47.210
But the 10th time I meet
him, I just go, hey, Neto,

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:47.990
and that's it.

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:50.570
And so your actions want to
have that flexibility too.

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:52.803
And so by pulling
out your responses,

00:11:52.803 --> 00:11:54.470
you have to kind of
think about what you

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.540
want to label the responses.

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:59.180
After that, because you're
going to have that pulled out,

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:02.390
you're going to want to have
that even further separated,

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:05.550
where you have a
locales directory.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:09.140
And in there, you have your
files for every single language

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:13.370
using the same skeleton of your
JSON of your primary language,

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:15.470
which this one was in English.

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:18.860
And so that way, when you're
using a client library,

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:22.190
you can figure out which
language it's working with.

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:24.560
So that's the second step I did.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:30.830
After that, I used I18N, pulled
that in, as well as Moment.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:33.630
And what I had to do was just
do some configuration going,

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:36.330
hey, this is where my
locales are going to be.

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:40.160
And these are the fallbacks of
the different languages that I

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.090
might not have predefined.

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:43.890
So one of them is for Spanish.

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:47.990
And so we have Spanish,
which is ES419.

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:50.990
And then we also have
Spanish, which is ES-ES.

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:53.270
So 419 means it's Latin America.

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:55.760
ES-ES means that
it's in Spain, which

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.880
is very different Spanishes.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:12:59.150
I'm treating them the same.

00:12:59.150 --> 00:13:03.110
So I'm just saying, go to my
same Spanish file and use that.

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:05.600
But you can have further
granularity within that

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:06.500
as well.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:08.567
So that's the next thing I did.

00:13:08.567 --> 00:13:10.025
And then within my
code, I just had

00:13:10.025 --> 00:13:11.720
to do a little bit
of refactoring.

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:15.140
And so this is my
welcome intent.

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:20.180
And so what's happening here
is that when my user activates

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:21.950
the action, it will greet them.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:23.900
And so first thing
that happens is

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:25.280
I will localize
the conversation.

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:27.830
I will figure out,
where is the user from?

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:29.630
What is their locale,
like the language

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:32.480
they're using, which I get from
the conversational object that

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:34.850
gets passed back and
forth between the user

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:36.480
and me as a developer.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:37.830
So I localize it.

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:40.700
And then I do some funny
jazz about suggestion tips.

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:42.180
Don't worry about that.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:44.210
And then I have an
if statement asking

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:45.890
if I've seen that user before.

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:47.600
And if I have seen
them, I'm going

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:51.290
to give them a message
of the welcome back text.

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:55.340
And so you can see I'm using
I18N double underscore,

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:59.120
passing in that string of
the name of the response.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.973
And then it creates
that message for me

00:14:01.973 --> 00:14:03.140
in the appropriate language.

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:06.650
And then it will send
it using the conv.ask--

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:07.670
oh, you can't see that.

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:08.870
Let me go on the mouse.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:10.310
Maybe you can see it there.

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:13.220
Then we do-- can
we see this mouse?

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:13.830
Yeah?

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:14.330
Maybe?

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:15.500
Thumbs up, people can see.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:16.640
Thank you.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:18.650
And then you do it
here with conv.ask,

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:21.410
sending in that speech message.

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:23.390
And then the message
in text form.

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:24.770
I have two variations.

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:28.220
I have a message that's in
the audio and message that

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:32.030
is in just text format
because my audio text actually

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.430
has natural line
breaks that I've put in

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:36.010
to make it sound more natural.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:37.810
And I've actually
messed with the grammar.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:40.670
And so instead of asking
questions with a question mark,

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:42.140
I'm asking questions
with a period.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:45.488
Because it changes how the
audio is heard as well.

00:14:45.488 --> 00:14:47.030
And so I did that
for every language,

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:48.890
tweaking the grammar slightly.

00:14:48.890 --> 00:14:51.252
I did ask for a lot of
help from co-workers.

00:14:51.252 --> 00:14:53.210
I asked a lot of help
from Neto to translate it

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:54.210
into Portuguese.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:55.100
So thank you so much.

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:55.808
NETO MARIN: Yeah.

00:14:55.808 --> 00:14:58.820
Imagine, for example, sometimes
you on this [INAUDIBLE] side.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:01.500
Imagine you're having
a conversation.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.728
You're not building
voice comments.

00:15:03.728 --> 00:15:04.520
They are different.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:05.900
Voice comments are one thing.

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:08.190
Conversations are another thing.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:11.480
So the point is, when you
are providing information

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:14.880
through voice, you need
sometimes to give more detail.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:19.390
So for example, [INAUDIBLE]
to show some example

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:22.580
and give you some example,
from the visual perspective,

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:26.420
this example can be a
suggestion chip on the response.

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:30.620
But this won't be audible
for the user that doesn't

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:33.230
have a screen available.

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:36.322
So we need to give the
example in the speech part.

00:15:36.322 --> 00:15:37.780
So this is the part
that's when you

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.990
have to separate the
speech between the text

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.670
and why you should
separate them.

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:42.450
JESSICA EARLEY-CHA: Definitely.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.325
And it was actually
really simple to localize

00:15:44.325 --> 00:15:48.250
because literally, it's the
same code, except I've changed.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:51.872
I've added this I18.N
double underscore.

00:15:51.872 --> 00:15:53.830
And that's the only thing
once I pulled it out.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.910
So making your action localized,
when it comes to the code part,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:58.340
it's super easy.

00:15:58.340 --> 00:16:00.430
The translation is a
little bit different.

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:02.138
So hopefully you have
really good friends

00:16:02.138 --> 00:16:05.290
that speak multiple languages
to help you on that.

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:06.610
And we have another.

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:09.790
We have Sphero, which is
a fantastic IoT action.

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:11.070
NETO MARIN: Yeah, exactly.

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:14.900
One of our jobs, as developer
advocates, that I tend to say

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.050
is the most fun
job in the world,

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:19.750
we have to play with
some toys, some things

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:21.550
to study, right, to work.

00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:24.190
This is the good part of
being a developer advocate.

00:16:24.190 --> 00:16:26.320
And one challenge
is how you can--

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:28.350
imagine we are
building actions that

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.310
are going to run on devices.

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:33.490
But if you have a
startup or have an idea

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:35.500
and you are building
out hardware,

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:36.970
or any other
appliance, and you'd

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:40.180
like to make it work with
the Google Assistant, how

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:41.500
it can work?

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.170
We have, for example, the
home automation framework

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:45.400
with Home Graph.

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:47.170
But if it's not for
home automation,

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:50.510
if I have, like,
this robot, I want

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:51.970
to control this over voice.

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:55.090
And this idea came
when my daughter

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:59.920
saw this toy in a
shopping and I said,

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.560
yeah, looks like a nice toy.

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:06.700
But I had to find the
adult excuse to buy.

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:08.560
So I said, yeah,
maybe it's good so I

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:10.720
can try with Google Assistant.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:16.060
And I realized it when she was
trying to control the Sphero.

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:19.420
And she started yelling at
this Sphero, stop, stop.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:20.619
I said, cool.

00:17:20.619 --> 00:17:23.380
I think it's easier to
control this over voice

00:17:23.380 --> 00:17:28.870
than using a smartphone
or using a joystick.

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:30.630
OK, joystick could
be cool, but OK.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:31.490
It's another thing.

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:34.030
So what I decided
to do is to showcase

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:36.560
how you could integrate
any kind of hardware.

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:40.440
So in this case specifically,
this little ball

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:44.170
is using BLI,
Bluetooth Low Energy.

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:47.590
So what I have here,
it's a Raspberry Pi.

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:50.860
And I'm using a Raspberry
Pi for the reason

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:54.580
that I want to have something on
my desk that I can try, deploy.

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:57.520
But you can, for example,
build an Android app that

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:00.490
connects through the Bluetooth
and run in your phone

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:02.050
if you want.

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:06.560
So here, we have a code that,
using the [INAUDIBLE] Node.JS

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:10.510
library, is sending comments
to control this Sphero.

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:12.250
And you know,
nothing new, right?

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:15.100
I'm just using the
pre-existing API

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:19.990
to send comments over Bluetooth
and make the ball run, right?

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:27.070
But if I say, OK, G, talk
to roll ball and hey,

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:31.600
go forward, go backward,
turn left, turn right.

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.450
So then we decide to do this.

00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:38.050
I was planning to show live,
